3. Subsystem Deep-Dive

3.1 IMU + AHRS (TDK ICM-42688-P + MMC5983MA)

  - Physical Input: Dynamic tri-axial acceleration up to +/- 16g, rotational
    rates up to +/- 2000 deg/s, and Earth-magnetic field flux down to
    micro-Tesla resolution sampled at 400 Hz.
  - Processed Output: High-rate 6-DOF orientation quaternion, filtered Euler
    angles (pitch, roll, yaw), high-frequency shock/vibration profiles, and
    vertical chassis displacement.
  - Control Effect:
    1.  Pitch/Roll Limit Trigger: If the robot encounters a mud sinkhole causing
        pitch or roll to breach 20 deg, high-rate power cutoff engages to
        prevent rollover.
    2.  Sinkage Detection: Integrating vertical acceleration (a_z - g) detects
        when the wheels have dug down and the chassis is resting flat on the mud
        (belly hung).
    3.  UWB Mast Gimbal Compensation: Mathematically rotates the dual UWB
        antenna vector into the inertial horizontal plane, preventing pitch/roll
        changes from distorting the calculated horizontal Angle-of-Arrival.

3.2 True Ground Speed Radar (Infineon BGT60TR13C 60GHz)

  - Physical Input: High-frequency 60GHz radio chirps reflected off the solid
    soil floor beneath muddy standing water.
  - Processed Output: Direct Doppler-derived true velocity vector (v_true).
  - Control Effect:
    1.  Slip Ratio Regulation: The controller compares true speed against wheel
        speed: s = 1 - (v_true / (w * r)).
    2.  Closed-Loop Traction Action: If s > 0.6, wheel torque is throttled back.
        Unlike dry asphalt traction control (which maintains slip around 0.15),
        wet paddy mud requires low-RPM high-torque crawling to prevent soil
        liquefaction.
    3.  Dead Reckoning & Gatekeeper: If the UWB ranging sensor suggests the
        robot moved forward by 1 meter in 50 ms due to an RF multipath glitch,
        the radar velocity flags this as physically impossible and rejects the
        UWB update.

3.3 Row Tracking (Hall-Effect Whiskers + VL53L8CX ToF)

  - Physical Input: Mechanical deflection of flexible carbon-fiber tactile
    whiskers brushing against sturdy rice hills, combined with 64-zone (8x8)
    infrared time-of-flight distance returns from the canopy.
  - Processed Output: Continuous signed lateral displacement error (e_y)
    representing distance from the crop furrow center.
  - Control Effect:
    1.  Proportional-Derivative Row Centering: Feeds directly into differential
        wheel speeds: Delta_w = Kp * e_y + Kd * (de_y / dt).
    2.  Zero-Latency Crop Avoidance: If whisker deflection reaches maximum
        mechanical limit on one side, an immediate steering override pulses the
        opposing motor to prevent wheel cleats from crushing the rice root base.

3.4 Load & Effort (INA240 Current Monitors + Tow Hitch Strain Gauge)

  - Physical Input: Phase current draws across all 4 brushless hub motors, plus
    resistive foil strain-gauge bridge output on the tow pin.
  - Processed Output: Soil resistance torque (tau_mud) in N*m and hitch cable
    pulling tension (F_pull) in Newtons.
  - Control Effect:
    1.  Active Tether Tension Control:
          - If F_pull < 20 N, the winch motor automatically winds in line to
            keep the tether off the muddy ground.
          - If 20 N <= F_pull <= 350 N, tension is nominal for cooperative
            formation towing.
          - If F_pull >= 400 N, the winch brake slips or disengages to protect
            cable integrity and prevent structural hitch shearing.
    2.  Tethered Tow Coordination: When a trailing unit registers extreme soil
        torque while stationary, the lead robot increases its forward tractive
        effort to tow the trailing unit out of the mud trap.

3.5 Swarm Ranging & Angle-of-Arrival (Dual Qorvo DWM3000 UWB)

  - Physical Input: Timestamped RF pulse packets received across two matched
    ceramic antennas separated by a fixed baseline on the top mast.
  - Processed Output: Inter-robot distance (d_ij) with +/- 5 cm accuracy and
    Phase Difference of Arrival (PDoA) yielding bearing angle (AoA).
  - Control Effect:
    1.  Longitudinal Convoy Spacing: Regulates cruise throttle to maintain a set
        following distance behind the lead robot.
    2.  Emergency Collision Envelope: If d_ij < 1.0 m, emergency braking is
        commanded across all drive channels.
    3.  Cooperative Pull Alignment: When pulling a neighbor via winch, the lead
        unit aligns its drive axis along the measured AoA bearing vector to pull
        directly inline with the cable.

3.6 Swarm Communications (Wi-Fi HaLow 802.11ah / ESP-NOW)

  - Physical Input: Long-range sub-1GHz radio packets capable of penetrating
    high-moisture foliage.
  - Processed Output: Decoded state packets containing peer health metrics, slip
    flags, load states, and LLM consensus tokens.
  - Control Effect:
    1.  Distributed Fault Triage: If any robot broadcasts SLIP_CRITICAL or
        PITCH_TRAP, the entire quintet transitions to a cooperative recovery
        mode (lead unit stops, follower prepares winch line).
    2.  LLM AgentVerse Coordination: Asynchronous exchange of high-level task
        allocations across the mid-level onboard computers (Raspberry Pi 5 /
        Jetson Orin Nano).

4. Compute Distribution Hierarchy

  - Low-Level Reflex Loop (STM32H7 @ 200 Hz):
      - Direct SPI/ADC sampling of IMU, mmWave radar, tactile whiskers, and
        strain gauges.
      - Real-time slip calculation, row-centering PD output, and safety-critical
        motor shutoffs.
  - Mid-Level Swarm & Agent Loop (Raspberry Pi 5 / Jetson Orin Nano):
      - Ingests filtered state vectors from STM32 over high-speed UART/CAN.
      - Encapsulates state data into Sub-1GHz packets for Wi-Fi HaLow / ESP-NOW
        transmission.
      - Executes asynchronous multi-agent coordination (AgentVerse LLM client)
        for field-level path planning and formation reassignment.
